::CW_U06_L01_Q01::Which portfolio selection reason is strongest?{
~A. I chose it because it was first in my folder.#Incorrect. File order does not prove craft value.
=B. I chose it because the revised dialogue shows clearer conflict between the characters.#Correct. This connects selection to craft growth.
~C. I chose it because it is short.#Incorrect. Length alone is not evidence.
~D. I chose it because I did not want to read another piece.#Incorrect. This is not a craft rationale.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q02::Which item is evidence in a craft rationale?{
~A. A vague feeling that the piece is nice.#Incorrect. Evidence must be specific.
~B. A random page number.#Incorrect. Page number alone is not craft evidence.
~C. A statement that writing happened.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=D. A before-and-after revision showing a stronger final image.#Correct. This is specific evidence of growth.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q03::A writer changed "I was afraid" to "I counted each floorboard between me and the door." What craft growth does this show?{
=A. Showing emotion through action and image.#Correct. The revision turns direct emotion into a concrete moment.
~B. Avoiding imagery.#Incorrect. The revision adds imagery.
~C. Removing reader effect.#Incorrect. The revision strengthens reader effect.
~D. Changing genre randomly.#Incorrect. The genre is not the issue.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q04::Which rationale is too vague?{
~A. The revised ending lets the reader infer the speaker's grief.#Incorrect. This is specific.
~B. The dialogue revision clarifies conflict.#Incorrect. This names craft improvement.
=C. This piece is good and I like it.#Correct. This gives opinion without evidence.
~D. The stanza break slows the final image.#Incorrect. This explains reader effect.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q05::Which three-part structure best fits a craft rationale?{
=A. Selection claim, evidence, reader effect.#Correct. This structure explains why the piece belongs in the portfolio.
~B. Title, date, file size.#Incorrect. These do not explain craft.
~C. Summary, summary, summary.#Incorrect. Summary alone is not a rationale.
~D. Apology, complaint, unrelated detail.#Incorrect. This does not support selection.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q06::A portfolio piece has strong imagery but no revision evidence. What should the writer add to the rationale?{
~A. A random compliment.#Incorrect. The rationale needs evidence.
~B. A claim that evidence is unnecessary.#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
~C. A statement about font color only.#Incorrect. Font color does not prove revision growth.
=D. A specific change, feedback note, or craft decision that proves growth.#Correct. This supplies evidence for the rationale.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q07::Which sentence best explains reader effect?{
~A. I made changes.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. The repeated image helps readers feel the speaker waiting even after the chance to leave passes.#Correct. This explains how the craft choice affects readers.
~C. The piece has words.#Incorrect. This does not explain effect.
~D. I wrote it last week.#Incorrect. Date written is not reader effect.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q08::Which selection should a writer question?{
~A. A piece that shows revision from telling to showing.#Incorrect. This may be a strong selection.
~B. A piece with clear evidence of improved format.#Incorrect. This may support the portfolio.
=C. A piece chosen only because it required no effort.#Correct. A portfolio piece should show craft value or growth.
~D. A piece that demonstrates stronger dialogue after feedback.#Incorrect. This is a valid reason.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q09::A craft rationale says, "I selected this because it is good." What is missing?{
~A. A longer title only.#Incorrect. The issue is missing evidence.
~B. A complaint about feedback.#Incorrect. That would not help.
~C. A random summary.#Incorrect. Summary alone is not enough.
=D. Specific evidence and explanation of reader effect.#Correct. The claim needs support.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q10::Which answer best uses feedback evidence?{
=A. Peer feedback showed the ending was too direct, so I revised the final sentence into an image that suggests hesitation.#Correct. This connects feedback, revision, and effect.
~B. I ignored all feedback.#Incorrect. This does not use evidence.
~C. Feedback exists somewhere.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~D. I changed everything for no reason.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q11::Which is the best reason to include a weaker first draft excerpt in a rationale?{
~A. To embarrass the writer.#Incorrect. The purpose is not shame.
=B. To show what changed and why the final version is stronger.#Correct. Before-and-after evidence can prove growth.
~C. To replace the final piece.#Incorrect. It supports the final piece.
~D. To avoid explaining craft.#Incorrect. It should support craft explanation.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q12::What should a writer do before writing the final rationale?{
=A. Choose evidence from the selected piece.#Correct. Evidence makes the rationale specific.
~B. Delete all revision notes.#Incorrect. Revision notes may be useful evidence.
~C. Decide evidence is unnecessary.#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
~D. Write only "I like it."#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q13::Which piece best shows portfolio growth?{
=A. A poem revised from direct emotion into purposeful imagery after feedback.#Correct. This shows revision and craft growth.
~B. A rough draft with no changes.#Incorrect. It does not show growth.
~C. A piece selected only because it is short.#Incorrect. Length is not growth.
~D. A story with no evidence or explanation.#Incorrect. The portfolio needs rationale.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q14::Which statement best explains why reader effect matters?{
=A. Reader effect shows how a craft choice changes the audience's experience.#Correct. This connects writing decisions to impact.
~B. Reader effect means readers are ignored.#Incorrect. It centers reader experience.
~C. Reader effect is only spelling.#Incorrect. It is broader than conventions.
~D. Reader effect means choosing randomly.#Incorrect. It requires purpose.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q15::Which evidence best supports a rationale about dialogue growth?{
=A. The final draft includes clearer word choices for each character.#Correct. This is specific dialogue evidence.
~B. The story has a title.#Incorrect. A title does not prove dialogue growth.
~C. The file is saved.#Incorrect. Saving a file is not craft evidence.
~D. The writer likes dialogue.#Incorrect. Preference is not evidence.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q16::Which rationale is strongest?{
~A. It is nice.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. I selected this script because adding speaker labels and shorter stage directions made the conflict easier to perform and understand.#Correct. This names selection, evidence, and reader effect.
~C. It is in my folder.#Incorrect. This does not explain craft value.
~D. I chose it randomly.#Incorrect. Random selection is weak.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q17::A writer says, "The line breaks are better now." What should the writer add?{
=A. Which line breaks changed and how they affect pacing or meaning.#Correct. Specific evidence and reader effect are needed.
~B. Nothing else.#Incorrect. The statement is incomplete.
~C. A random complaint.#Incorrect. That does not explain craft.
~D. A denial that format matters.#Incorrect. Format can affect meaning.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q18::Which answer best distinguishes summary from rationale?{
=A. Summary retells what happens; rationale explains why the piece was selected and what craft evidence supports it.#Correct. This separates content recap from craft reasoning.
~B. Summary and rationale are always identical.#Incorrect. They serve different purposes.
~C. Rationale never uses evidence.#Incorrect. Rationale requires evidence.
~D. Summary is always stronger than craft reasoning.#Incorrect. The portfolio requires rationale.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q19::Which response best handles a piece the writer likes but cannot explain?{
~A. Include it without evidence.#Incorrect. The rationale would be weak.
=B. Look for specific craft evidence or choose another piece that better shows growth.#Correct. Portfolio choices must be supported.
~C. Delete the whole portfolio.#Incorrect. That is unnecessary.
~D. Claim readers do not need reasons.#Incorrect. The assignment requires rationale.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q20::Which phrase should be replaced in a craft rationale?{
~A. "The repeated image..."#Incorrect. This can introduce evidence.
~B. "After feedback..."#Incorrect. This can introduce revision evidence.
=C. "It is good."#Correct. This is vague and needs evidence.
~D. "The reader can infer..."#Incorrect. This explains reader effect.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q21::Which is the best checkpoint submission?{
=A. A selected piece title, selection claim, two pieces of evidence, and reader-effect explanation.#Correct. This matches the lesson checkpoint.
~B. A title only.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~C. A list of favorite pieces with no explanation.#Incorrect. Portfolio selection needs rationale.
~D. A plot summary only.#Incorrect. Summary does not replace rationale.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q22::What makes revision evidence stronger than general praise?{
=A. It points to a specific change and explains why the final piece improved.#Correct. This proves growth.
~B. It avoids details.#Incorrect. Evidence needs details.
~C. It says the piece is nice.#Incorrect. That is general praise.
~D. It ignores reader effect.#Incorrect. Reader effect matters.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q23::A rationale claims a story shows growth in structure. Which evidence best supports the claim?{
=A. The writer added a transition that clarifies the flashback.#Correct. This supports structural growth.
~B. The writer likes blue covers.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~C. The story was easy to find.#Incorrect. This is not structural evidence.
~D. The file name is short.#Incorrect. This does not show structure.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q24::Which answer shows weak selection thinking?{
~A. I selected this because feedback helped me revise the ending.#Incorrect. This is strong.
=B. I selected this because I did not want to think about another piece.#Correct. This is not a craft-based reason.
~C. I selected this because the imagery improved after revision.#Incorrect. This is strong.
~D. I selected this because the final format supports the poem's pacing.#Incorrect. This is strong.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q25::Which response best shows Lesson 1 mastery?{
=A. I can choose a portfolio piece using craft evidence, revision evidence, and reader-effect explanation.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson skill.
~B. I can choose a piece because it is easy.#Incorrect. Ease is not a craft rationale.
~C. I can avoid evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
~D. I can write only vague praise.#Incorrect. Vague praise is insufficient.
}
